[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Java Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## What Java is\nJava is a statically typed, object-oriented programming language. Developers write source code in `.java` files and compile it into bytecode stored in `.class` files. The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) executes this bytecode on supported operating systems.\n\n## Compile and run\n```bash\njavac Hello.java\njava Hello\n```\n`javac` compiles source code. The `java` launcher starts the JVM and loads the requested class.\n\n## Platform independence\nJava bytecode is not tied to one processor instruction set. A compatible JVM translates or compiles bytecode for its host platform. This is the basis of Java's write-once, run-anywhere approach.\n\n## Important distinction\nThe JDK provides development tools, the JRE concept describes the runtime components needed to run applications, and the JVM executes bytecode. Modern JDK distributions commonly package the runtime directly.\n\n## Practical use\nA developer can compile a Spring Boot service on Windows and deploy the resulting application to a Linux server with a compatible Java runtime."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## Types and variables\nJava requires variables to have declared types. Primitive types such as `int`, `long`, `boolean`, and `double` store simple values. Reference variables refer to objects.\n\n```java\nint retries = 3;\nString serviceName = \"payment-api\";\nboolean enabled = true;\n```\n\n## Classes and objects\nA class defines fields and methods. An object is an instance created from that class. Constructors initialize new objects, while methods define their behaviour.\n\n## Methods and control flow\nMethods group reusable operations. Conditions, loops, and exception handling control how those operations execute.\n\n## Pass-by-value\nJava always passes arguments by value. For object arguments, the copied value is the reference; a method can modify the referenced object's state but cannot replace the caller's reference variable.\n\n## Practical example\nA deployment utility might use a `DeploymentConfig` class to hold a namespace, image tag, and replica count, then validate those values before deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Architecture and Component Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Architecture and Component Responsibilities\n\n## Main components\nThe Java development and execution environment contains several cooperating components.\n\n- **JDK:** Development kit containing tools such as `javac`, `java`, and diagnostic utilities.\n- **Compiler:** Converts Java source code into JVM bytecode.\n- **Class loader:** Locates and loads class definitions when required.\n- **Bytecode verifier:** Checks class-file structure and bytecode safety constraints.\n- **JVM runtime:** Provides execution, memory management, and runtime services.\n- **Execution engine:** Interprets bytecode and may compile frequently executed code into native machine code using JIT compilation.\n\n## Runtime memory\nThe heap stores objects and arrays. Each thread has its own JVM stack containing frames for method calls. The method area holds class-level runtime structures. Metaspace is a common HotSpot implementation area for class metadata.\n\n## Architecture flow\nSource code is compiled, classes are loaded and linked, and methods execute through the JVM execution engine.\n\n## Practical context\nWhen a Java API starts in a container, its JVM loads application classes, allocates objects, executes request handlers, and manages memory while serving traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Execution Lifecycle",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Execution Lifecycle\n\n## From source code to execution\nA Java program passes through distinct stages before and during execution.\n\n1. **Write:** Developers create source files such as `Main.java`.\n2. **Compile:** `javac Main.java` checks the source and produces bytecode in `Main.class`.\n3. **Launch:** The `java` command starts a JVM process.\n4. **Load:** Class loaders locate and load required classes.\n5. **Link:** The JVM verifies bytecode, prepares class-level structures, and resolves references as required.\n6. **Initialize:** Static initializers and static field initializers execute when the class is initialized.\n7. **Execute:** The JVM interprets bytecode and may JIT-compile frequently executed methods.\n\n## Example\n```bash\njavac Main.java\njava Main\n```\n\n## Operational relevance\nA service can fail at compilation, class loading, initialization, or runtime. These are different failure stages and require different evidence.\n\nFor example, `ClassNotFoundException` commonly indicates an explicit class-loading lookup failure, while `NoClassDefFoundError` indicates that a required class definition could not be used during execution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Configuration and Implementation",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Configuration and Implementation\n\n## Java configuration\nA Java application depends on a compatible JDK or runtime, application configuration, and its required libraries. The `JAVA_HOME` environment variable commonly identifies the installed JDK directory.\n\nCheck the active tools:\n\n```bash\njava -version\njavac -version\n```\n\n## Compile with a specific release\n```bash\njavac --release 17 Main.java\n```\nThe `--release` option targets the specified Java platform API and class-file version, subject to the installed compiler supporting that release.\n\n## Runtime options\nJVM options can configure heap limits and diagnostics. For example:\n\n```bash\njava -Xms256m -Xmx512m -jar app.jar\n```\n\n`-Xms` sets the initial heap size and `-Xmx` sets the maximum heap size.\n\n## Implementation practice\nKeep environment-specific settings outside source code. Use environment variables or a configuration system for deployment-specific values, and never commit passwords or access tokens to source control."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Code Structure and Design",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Code Structure and Design\n\n## Organizing Java code\nA Java application is commonly organized into packages that group related classes. Package names usually follow a reversed domain convention, such as `com.example.orders`.\n\nA class should have a clear responsibility. Separate business rules, data access, API handling, and configuration instead of placing all application logic in one large class.\n\n## Example structure\n```text\nsrc/\n  main/\n    java/com/example/orders/\n      Order.java\n      OrderService.java\n      OrderRepository.java\n      OrderController.java\n  test/\n    java/com/example/orders/\n      OrderServiceTest.java\n```\n\n## Encapsulation\nKeep internal state private and expose operations through appropriate methods. Use interfaces where they provide a meaningful boundary between components.\n\n## Maintainability\nPrefer small cohesive classes, descriptive names, immutable data where practical, and explicit dependencies.\n\n## DevOps relevance\nA predictable project structure makes builds, automated tests, code reviews, and CI pipeline configuration easier to maintain."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Practical Development Workflow",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Practical Development Workflow\n\n## Typical workflow\nA Java developer usually follows a repeatable cycle: inspect the repository, select the required JDK, implement a change, compile, test, package, and review the result.\n\n## Maven example\n```bash\nmvn clean test\nmvn package\n```\n\n`clean` removes generated build output, `test` runs the configured test phase, and `package` creates the configured artifact after earlier lifecycle phases succeed.\n\n## Version control\nUse Git branches for isolated changes. Review the diff before committing and avoid adding generated output, local IDE settings, or secrets.\n\n## CI integration\nA pipeline can check out the repository, install or select the required JDK, resolve dependencies, run tests, and publish the JAR file.\n\n## Reliable practice\nKeep build instructions documented and reproducible. Pin important plugin and dependency versions, and ensure that local and CI environments use compatible Java versions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Testing and Verification",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Testing and Verification\n\n## Testing layers\nJava projects commonly use unit tests for individual methods or classes, integration tests for collaborating components, and end-to-end tests for complete application workflows.\n\n## Example with JUnit\n```java\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\n\nclass CalculatorTest {\n    @Test\n    void addsTwoNumbers() {\n        assertEquals(5, 2 + 3);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Build verification\nFor a Maven project, run:\n\n```bash\nmvn clean verify\n```\n\nThe `verify` phase runs the lifecycle phases before it, including configured tests and verification checks.\n\n## Useful checks\nVerify the supported Java version, compilation result, test reports, dependency resolution, and packaged artifact.\n\n## CI/CD relevance\nA pipeline should fail when required tests fail. Do not publish a release artifact merely because compilation succeeded; test and packaging checks provide additional confidence."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Common Errors and Failure Modes",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Common Errors and Failure Modes\n\n## Compilation failures\nSyntax errors, incompatible types, missing imports, and unavailable dependencies can prevent compilation. Read the first meaningful compiler diagnostic and inspect the referenced file and line.\n\n## Runtime failures\nA `NullPointerException` occurs when code dereferences a null reference. An `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` indicates an invalid array index. An `OutOfMemoryError` indicates that the JVM could not satisfy an allocation under its memory constraints.\n\n## Classpath problems\nMissing or conflicting dependencies can cause class-loading and linkage errors. Check the packaged artifact and runtime classpath rather than assuming the source code is incorrect.\n\n## Environment mismatch\nAn application compiled for a newer Java release may not run on an older runtime.\n\n## Troubleshooting approach\nCapture the complete error, identify whether failure occurred during compilation, startup, or request processing, and reproduce it with the same Java version and configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Debugging and Diagnostics",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Debugging and Diagnostics\n\n## Start with evidence\nWhen a Java process fails, collect the exception, stack trace, application logs, Java version, startup arguments, and relevant environment configuration.\n\n## Inspect the process\n```bash\njcmd -l\njcmd <pid> VM.version\njcmd <pid> Thread.print\n```\n\nReplace `<pid>` with the actual Java process ID. `jcmd` can request supported diagnostic information from a running JVM.\n\n## Stack traces\nRead the exception type and follow the stack trace from the application code outward. The first relevant application frame often identifies where the failure surfaced.\n\n## Debugging tools\nUse an IDE debugger for breakpoints and variable inspection. For production incidents, prefer controlled diagnostic commands and approved observability tooling.\n\n## Safety\nThread dumps and diagnostic output may contain internal class names, paths, or application details. Store and share them according to operational security policies."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Performance and Optimization",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Performance and Optimization\n\n## Measure before optimizing\nJava performance depends on workload, allocation rate, garbage collection, thread behaviour, I/O, and application design. Establish a baseline before changing JVM settings.\n\n## Common bottlenecks\nHigh CPU can indicate expensive computation or repeated work. High allocation rates can increase garbage-collection activity. Slow database or network calls can make an application appear CPU-idle while requests remain slow.\n\n## Useful diagnostics\n```bash\njcmd <pid> Thread.print\njcmd <pid> GC.heap_info\n```\n\nUse supported JVM monitoring tools to inspect thread activity and heap behaviour. A profiler can identify hot methods more reliably than guessing.\n\n## Optimization practices\nReduce unnecessary object creation, use efficient data structures, avoid blocking critical request threads, and tune connection pools based on measured demand.\n\n## Production caution\nDo not increase heap limits blindly. Container memory limits must account for heap, metaspace, thread stacks, direct buffers, and other native memory."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Security Considerations",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Security Considerations\n\n## Secure Java development\nTreat external input as untrusted. Validate request data, use parameterized database queries, and apply correct authorization checks at service boundaries.\n\n## Dependency security\nThird-party libraries can contain known vulnerabilities. Track dependency versions, review security advisories, and update affected packages through a tested change process.\n\n## Secrets\nDo not hard-code passwords, private keys, or API tokens in Java source files or Git repositories. Supply secrets through an approved secret manager or protected runtime configuration.\n\n## Transport and permissions\nUse TLS for sensitive network communication. Run Java services with only the operating-system permissions they need.\n\n## Runtime hygiene\nKeep the JDK and runtime patched. Restrict access to diagnostic endpoints and avoid exposing sensitive data in logs or exception responses.\n\n## CI/CD practice\nInclude dependency scanning, secret detection, tests, and artifact provenance checks in the delivery pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-13",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Production Integration",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Production Integration\n\n## Packaging\nA Java service is commonly packaged as a JAR file. Framework-based applications may create executable JARs that include application dependencies.\n\n```bash\nmvn clean package\njava -jar target/application.jar\n```\n\n## Deployment integration\nA CI pipeline should build and test the application, publish an immutable artifact, and deploy that exact artifact to the target environment.\n\n## Container deployment\nA container image needs a compatible Java runtime, the application artifact, required certificates, and appropriate runtime configuration. Set memory limits with awareness of the container's total memory budget.\n\n## Observability\nSend structured application logs to a central platform. Expose approved health and metrics endpoints, and monitor request latency, error rates, JVM memory, and process health.\n\n## Reliability\nUse readiness and liveness checks appropriately, configure graceful shutdown, and make deployment rollback possible when a release fails verification."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-1-section-14",
        "title": "Java Fundamentals — Operational Best Practices",
        "content": "# Java Fundamentals — Operational Best Practices\n\n## Standardize the runtime\nChoose a supported Java release and use a consistent version across developer machines, CI agents, build images, and production environments.\n\n## Reproducible builds\nPin dependency and plugin versions, use a dependency lock or verification strategy where supported, and retain build logs and artifact metadata.\n\n## Resource management\nSet JVM heap limits with container memory in mind. Monitor garbage collection, thread counts, CPU, and memory rather than relying only on process uptime.\n\n## Release safety\nTest artifacts before promotion, deploy immutable versions, use health checks, and maintain a rollback procedure.\n\n## Security and maintenance\nPatch the runtime and dependencies, protect secrets, run services with least privilege, and restrict administrative endpoints.\n\n## Incident readiness\nDocument startup commands, configuration sources, log locations, health checks, and diagnostic procedures. These practices make Java services easier to operate consistently in production."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Java Project Structure",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## What a Java project contains\nA Java project organizes source code, resources, tests, build configuration, and generated artifacts. Its structure allows developers and build tools to locate the correct files consistently.\n\n## Standard Maven layout\n```text\nmy-application/\n  pom.xml\n  src/\n    main/\n      java/\n      resources/\n    test/\n      java/\n      resources/\n```\n\n`src/main/java` contains production Java source files. `src/main/resources` contains application resources such as configuration files. Test code and test resources belong under `src/test`.\n\n## Package and directory relationship\nJava packages organize classes into namespaces. Build tools expect source files in the configured source roots, with package declarations matching the intended namespace.\n\n## Runtime relevance\nThe build process compiles source files, copies resources, and packages classes and dependencies into an artifact. A correct project layout makes this process predictable.\n\n## Practical example\nA Spring Boot service can place its application entry point under `com.example.orders`, with controllers, services, and repositories in clearly separated subpackages."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## Source roots and packages\nA source root tells the build system where Java source files begin. Package declarations determine the fully qualified names of classes. For example, `com.example.billing.InvoiceService` identifies a class in the `com.example.billing` package.\n\n## Main and test code\nProduction code belongs in the main source set. Unit tests belong in the test source set and are compiled and executed by the test framework and build plugins.\n\n## Resources\nConfiguration templates, SQL scripts, and other non-Java files are placed in resource directories. Maven copies main resources into the application output during the build lifecycle.\n\n## Generated output\nMaven normally writes compiled classes and packaged artifacts under `target/`. This directory is build output, not the authoritative source of the application.\n\n## Why separation matters\nKeeping source, tests, resources, and generated files separate supports repeatable builds, clean version control, and reliable CI execution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Architecture and Component Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Architecture and Component Responsibilities\n\n## Project layers\nA maintainable Java application separates responsibilities instead of putting all code into one package.\n\n- **Controller or API layer:** Receives external requests and returns responses.\n- **Service layer:** Implements business operations and coordinates application workflows.\n- **Repository layer:** Reads and writes persistent data.\n- **Domain layer:** Represents business concepts and rules.\n- **Configuration layer:** Defines application wiring and environment integration.\n\n## Example package layout\n```text\ncom.example.inventory\n  controller/\n  service/\n  repository/\n  domain/\n  config/\n```\n\n## Build responsibilities\nThe build descriptor defines dependencies, plugins, compiler settings, test execution, and packaging. Maven's `pom.xml` is the central project configuration file.\n\n## Architectural benefit\nA clear boundary makes it easier to test business logic without starting the entire application and reduces accidental coupling between web, database, and domain code.\n\n## Production relevance\nWell-separated modules simplify maintenance, security reviews, troubleshooting, and future service changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Execution Lifecycle",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Execution Lifecycle\n\n## From repository to running application\nA Java project moves through a build and runtime lifecycle.\n\n1. The developer checks out the repository.\n2. Maven reads `pom.xml` and resolves required plugins and dependencies.\n3. The compiler compiles files from the configured source roots.\n4. Resources are copied into the output directory.\n5. Test sources are compiled and tests are executed.\n6. Packaging creates a JAR or WAR artifact.\n7. The runtime starts the application using the packaged artifact.\n\n## Example\n```bash\nmvn clean verify\njava -jar target/application.jar\n```\n\nThe exact artifact filename depends on the project's Maven coordinates and build configuration.\n\n## CI/CD workflow\nA pipeline should build from a clean checkout, execute verification, retain test reports, and publish the resulting artifact.\n\n## Failure diagnosis\nA missing source directory, incorrect package, unresolved dependency, or failed test can stop a different lifecycle phase. Identify the failing phase before changing project files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Configuration and Implementation",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Configuration and Implementation\n\n## Maven project descriptor\nThe `pom.xml` defines project coordinates, dependencies, properties, build plugins, and other build settings.\n\n```xml\n<properties>\n    <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>\n    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>\n</properties>\n```\n\nThe compiler release setting selects the Java language and platform API level when supported by the installed Maven compiler plugin and JDK.\n\n## Dependency declaration\nDependencies should specify appropriate versions and scopes. Avoid adding libraries without understanding their purpose and transitive dependencies.\n\n## Build plugins\nUse the Maven compiler, test, and packaging plugins with documented versions and settings. Plugin configuration should be consistent between developer machines and CI agents.\n\n## Environment-specific configuration\nDo not hard-code production endpoints or credentials into the build descriptor. Keep deployment configuration separate from build configuration.\n\n## Verification\nRun `mvn help:effective-pom` when you need to inspect the combined Maven configuration inherited from parent POMs and profiles."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Code Structure and Design",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Code Structure and Design\n\n## Organizing packages\nGroup classes by clear business capability or technical responsibility. Avoid a single package containing unrelated controllers, database code, configuration, and business rules.\n\n## Naming\nUse descriptive package, class, and method names. Java package names are conventionally lowercase, while class names use PascalCase.\n\n## Example\n```text\ncom.example.payments\n  api/\n  application/\n  domain/\n  infrastructure/\n```\n\nThis layout separates external interfaces, application workflows, business rules, and technical integrations.\n\n## Modular design\nA module should expose a small, intentional interface. Other modules should depend on that interface rather than reaching into internal implementation details.\n\n## Avoid overengineering\nDo not create empty packages or layers without a real responsibility. The right structure depends on project size and domain complexity.\n\n## Maintainability\nA developer should be able to locate a request handler, its business operation, and its data access path without searching through unrelated code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Practical Development Workflow",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Practical Development Workflow\n\n## Starting a project\nCreate the repository, select the Java version, initialize the Maven project descriptor, and establish the standard source and test directories.\n\n## Daily development\nImplement a focused change, add or update tests, run the relevant Maven lifecycle, inspect the diff, and commit only the intended source and configuration files.\n\n```bash\nmvn clean test\ngit status\ngit diff\n```\n\n## Team workflow\nUse a consistent project layout and document local setup requirements. Code reviews should check dependency changes, configuration handling, package boundaries, and test coverage.\n\n## CI workflow\nA build agent should use a clean checkout and the expected JDK. It should execute the same verification command used by developers and archive the generated artifact and test reports.\n\n## Practical discipline\nDo not manually edit generated files under `target/`. Change the source or build configuration and regenerate the output through Maven."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Testing and Verification",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Testing and Verification\n\n## Verify the layout\nConfirm that production source, test source, and resources are placed in their expected directories. Check that package declarations match the intended class names.\n\n## Maven verification\n```bash\nmvn clean test\nmvn verify\n```\n\n`test` executes configured tests. `verify` runs the lifecycle through the verification phase, including preceding phases.\n\n## Check test discovery\nIf tests are not running, inspect the test framework dependency and Surefire or Failsafe plugin configuration. Unit tests and integration tests may use different naming and execution conventions.\n\n## Artifact verification\nInspect the packaged JAR to confirm that expected classes and resources are included:\n\n```bash\njar tf target/application.jar\n```\n\nThe actual archive name depends on the project.\n\n## CI quality gate\nA successful compile alone is insufficient. Require the intended tests, packaging, and verification checks before publishing a release artifact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Common Errors and Failure Modes",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Common Errors and Failure Modes\n\n## Incorrect source placement\nA Java file outside the configured source roots may not be compiled. Move it into the correct directory or deliberately configure an additional source root.\n\n## Package mismatch\nA package declaration that does not match the expected namespace can cause confusing imports and class-loading problems. Check the fully qualified class name and directory layout.\n\n## Missing dependencies\nAn unresolved artifact may result from an incorrect version, repository configuration, network restriction, or dependency declaration.\n\n## Resource not found\nA resource stored outside the configured resource directories may not be included in the packaged artifact.\n\n## Generated output confusion\nEditing files inside `target/` is temporary because Maven can recreate that directory. Make changes in source files or build configuration.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nRun `mvn clean test`, inspect the first meaningful error, and verify the effective POM, source roots, dependency tree, and packaged archive."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Java Project Structure — Debugging and Diagnostics",
        "content": "# Java Project Structure — Debugging and Diagnostics\n\n## Inspect the build\nStart with the exact Maven command and the complete build output. Identify whether the failure occurred during dependency resolution, compilation, testing, or packaging.\n\n```bash\nmvn -version\nmvn help:effective-pom\nmvn dependency:tree\n```\n\nThese commands show the Maven and Java environment, the effective project configuration, and the resolved dependency hierarchy.\n\n## Inspect project files\nCheck `pom.xml`, package declarations, source directories, resource locations, and test naming conventions. Confirm that the expected files are tracked by Git.\n\n## Inspect the artifact\n```bash\njar tf target/application.jar\n```\nUse the result to determine whether required classes and resources were packaged.\n\n## CI diagnostics\nCompare the JDK version, Maven version, environment variables, and repository configuration between local and CI execution.\n\n## Safe troubleshooting\nMake one controlled change at a time. Preserve the original build log and avoid deleting source files or changing dependency versions without evidence."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Object-Oriented Programming",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Object-Oriented Programming — Language and Runtime Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Object-Oriented Programming — Language and Runtime Fundamentals\n\n## Concept and technical explanation\n\nThis section examines Language and Runtime Fundamentals as it applies specifically to Object-Oriented Programming. Understand\nthe underlying technology, the components involved, the inputs and outputs, and the\nconditions under which this feature is used in a real DevOps delivery environment.\n\n## Architecture and execution\n\nTrace the relevant component interactions. Identify the initiating component,\nprocessing component, configuration inputs, permissions, dependencies, resulting\nstate and observable outputs. Explain how the feature connects to the surrounding\npipeline stages.\n\n## Implementation\n\nImplement this concept using the actual technology and configuration relevant to\nObject-Oriented Programming. Use the appropriate source files, commands, YAML, JSON, scripts or platform\nconfiguration. Explain each important setting and why it is required.\n\n## Practical verification\n\nExecute the implementation in a controlled environment. Verify the expected result\nusing command output, logs, API responses, generated artifacts, platform status or\nruntime behaviour. Distinguish a successful command from a genuinely healthy result.\n\n## Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate realistic failures associated with Object-Oriented Programming and Language and Runtime Fundamentals. Collect\nevidence, identify the responsible component, explain the root cause, apply a targeted\ncorrection and repeat verification. Avoid blind retries and destructive recovery.\n\n## Production application\n\nExplain how this capability is used in a production DevOps workflow. Cover security,\nreliability, maintainability, performance, operational ownership and its effect on\ndownstream pipeline stages."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Object-Oriented Programming — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour",
        "content": "# Object-Oriented Programming — Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour\n\n## Concept and technical explanation\n\nThis section examines Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour as it applies specifically to Object-Oriented Programming. Understand\nthe underlying technology, the components involved, the inputs and outputs, and the\nconditions under which this feature is used in a real DevOps delivery environment.\n\n## Architecture and execution\n\nTrace the relevant component interactions. Identify the initiating component,\nprocessing component, configuration inputs, permissions, dependencies, resulting\nstate and observable outputs. Explain how the feature connects to the surrounding\npipeline stages.\n\n## Implementation\n\nImplement this concept using the actual technology and configuration relevant to\nObject-Oriented Programming. Use the appropriate source files, commands, YAML, JSON, scripts or platform\nconfiguration. Explain each important setting and why it is required.\n\n## Practical verification\n\nExecute the implementation in a controlled environment. Verify the expected result\nusing command output, logs, API responses, generated artifacts, platform status or\nruntime behaviour. Distinguish a successful command from a genuinely healthy result.\n\n## Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate realistic failures associated with Object-Oriented Programming and Core Concepts and Internal Behaviour. Collect\nevidence, identify the responsible component, explain the root cause, apply a targeted\ncorrection and repeat verification. Avoid blind retries and destructive recovery.\n\n## Production application\n\nExplain how this capability is used in a production DevOps workflow. Cover security,\nreliability, maintainability, performance, operational ownership and its effect on\ndownstream pipeline stages."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-1-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Object-Oriented Programming — Architecture and Component Responsibilities",
        "content": "# Object-Oriented Programming — Architecture and Component Responsibilities\n\n## Concept and technical explanation\n\nThis section examines Architecture and Component Responsibilities as it applies specifically to Object-Oriented Programming. Understand\nthe underlying technology, the components involved, the inputs and outputs, and the\nconditions under which this feature is used in a real DevOps delivery environment.\n\n## Architecture and execution\n\nTrace the relevant component interactions. Identify the initiating component,\nprocessing component, configuration inputs, permissions, dependencies, resulting\nstate and observable outputs. Explain how the feature connects to the surrounding\npipeline stages.\n\n## Implementation\n\nImplement this concept using the actual technology and configuration relevant to\nObject-Oriented Programming. Use the appropriate source files, commands, YAML, JSON, scripts or platform\nconfiguration. Explain each important setting and why it is required.\n\n## Practical verification\n\nExecute the implementation in a controlled environment. Verify the expected result\nusing command output, logs, API responses, generated artifacts, platform status or\nruntime behaviour. Distinguish a successful command from a genuinely healthy result.\n\n## Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate realistic failures associated with Object-Oriented Programming and Architecture and Component Responsibilities. Collect\nevidence, identify the responsible component, explain the root cause, apply a targeted\ncorrection and repeat verification. Avoid blind retries and destructive recovery.\n\n## Production application\n\nExplain how this capability is used in a production DevOps workflow. Cover security,\nreliability, maintainability, performance, operational ownership and its effect on\ndownstream pipeline stages."
